## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical tenancy controls in a NoSQL store; checks how to enforce data isolation with security rules, IAM, and network boundaries; assesses ability to design automated containment.

## Key Concepts
- Firestore security rules and tenant-scoped paths
- Per-tenant service accounts with least privilege
- Private Service Connect and VPC Service Controls
- CI/CD gate with synthetic tenants and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Firestore rules snippet (illustrative)
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    match /tenants/{tenantId}/{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null && request.auth.token.tenants.contains(tenantId);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for potential rule bypasses in CI?
- What are the trade-offs of using per-tenant collections vs single collection with tenant_id field?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical, end-to-end thinking for data lifecycle and cross-service cleanup in a real GCP setup.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-based data tagging and discovery across services
- Safe deletion strategies and audit trail
- Backup scrub/retention implications with CMEK
- IAM least privilege and cross-service permissions
- Validation and rollback planning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-deletion snippet (conceptual only)
async function purgeTenant(tenantId){
  await revokeTenantAccess(tenantId);
  await purgeCloudSql(tenantId);
  await deleteCloudStoragePrefix(tenantId);
  await deleteFirestoreDocs(tenantId);
  await purgePubSubResources(tenantId);
  await validateNoResidualData(tenantId);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- What if deletion fails on one service after others succeed?
- How would you automate periodic offboarding to ensure compliance and completeness?
